{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966813,"nonce":"C9sVgNKT6H7Gl//xSq/EjhSU8C8=","title":"cn.Dialog/Sheet/Drawer in the DSL — the signal is the overlay handle; keyed by signal, not","message":"The Rust overlay widgets are imperative: `dialog().show()` pushes onto the overlay stack and returns an `OverlayHandle` to close later. A `.blinc` source has nowhere to keep a handle, so the **signal is the handle** — set it and the overlay appears, clear it and it goes. Shared implementation in `blinc_cn_dsl`'s `modal` module; `cn.Dialog`, `cn.Sheet` and `cn.Drawer` all use it.\n\nThree things it has to get right, each of which failed first:\n\n- **The watcher must be subscribed.** Nothing else in a view depends on an overlay's `open` signal, so a plain element is built once and a write raises nothing until something unrelated drives a frame. It is a `Stateful` with `deps` on the signal that draws an empty `Div`.\n- **`show` must be idempotent.** The watcher re-renders whenever it is dirty; showing an already-open overlay stacks a second copy.\n- **The live handle lives outside the widget.** The extern-widget struct is rebuilt every frame, so a handle stored on it is empty next time and the overlay reopens once per frame.\n\n**Keyed by the signal's raw id, not by call site.** `current_call_id()` reads as **zero** in this path, which put every overlay in a program on one key: the first opened and the second silently never did. `CnInput` uses `CallSiteId` successfully, so call-site identity works on some paths and not others — check it rather than assume.\n\n`OverlayStack::close` unwinds everything **above** the target, so a sheet dismissed under a drawer takes the drawer with it. The unwound one does not reopen, because its own signal never changed and its watcher never re-ran — a polling watcher would raise it again next frame.\n\nTesting: overlay tests share one process-wide stack, so they must run as one test or count each other's modals. Count entries with `!exiting` — `close` marks an overlay exiting and leaves it in the stack while its motion plays out. See gotcha dsl test needs stateful notifier.","files":null}]}